# Blueprint form integration

Blueprint form controls are framework-agnostic. Compose fields with `BpFormSection` and form rows, then wire validation through your form library.

## Storybook and MCP

MCP: `guidelines-form` · components: `components-text-field`, `components-text-area`, `components-dropdown` · Storybook: `Components/Text field`, `Components/Text area`, `Components/Dropdown`, `Components/Form` → **Dialog form section**, **Scrolling form**, **Accordion-based form**, **Common input blocks**

## Related guidelines

- [`ai-text-field-guidelines.md`](./ai-text-field-guidelines.md) — validation and messaging props
- [`ai-dropdown-guidelines.md`](./ai-dropdown-guidelines.md) — composite dropdown behavior
- [`ai-layout-guidelines.md`](./ai-layout-guidelines.md) — form layout (`BpFormSection`, form rows), page shell, and CSS entry imports (`css` vs `prototype`)

## Integration pattern

- Compose fields inside `BpFormSection` and `bp-form-row` rows before wiring validation.
- Use native-field primitives with your form library's normal field registration or controlled APIs.
- Map form validation state to Blueprint presentation props (`hasError`, `message`, `messageType`).
- Use controller-style wrappers for composite controls that are not backed by a single native value element.

## Native-field controls

These components forward refs to the underlying native control and support controlled/uncontrolled input patterns:

- `BpTextField`
- `BpPasswordField`
- `BpTextFieldWithAction`
- `BpTextFieldWithSelection`
- `BpTextArea`
- `BpCheckbox`
- `BpRadioButton`
- `BpToggle`
- `BpSearch`
- `BpDropdownSearch`

## Validation mapping

Use your form library's field metadata to set Blueprint feedback props:

- `hasError`: boolean invalid state
- `message`: validation or helper text
- `messageType`: `"error" | "warning" | "information" | "success"` (most validation cases should use `"error"`)

Example mapping shape:

```ts
const invalid = Boolean(fieldState.error);
const message = fieldState.error?.message;
```

Then pass those values to the component.

## React Hook Form examples

### Register-style (`BpTextField`)

```tsx
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { BpTextField } from "@netapp/blueprint";

type FormValues = { name: string };

export function NameForm() {
  const {
    register,
    formState: { errors },
  } = useForm<FormValues>({
    defaultValues: { name: "" },
  });

  return (
    <BpTextField
      label="Name"
      {...register("name")}
      hasError={Boolean(errors.name)}
      message={errors.name?.message}
      messageType={errors.name ? "error" : undefined}
    />
  );
}
```

### Controller-style (`BpDropdown`)

`BpDropdown` is a composite control with value callbacks, so controller-style integration is preferred.

```tsx
import { Controller, useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { BpDropdown } from "@netapp/blueprint";

type FormValues = { tier?: string };

const options = [
  { value: "standard", label: "Standard" },
  { value: "premium", label: "Premium" },
];

export function TierForm() {
  const { control } = useForm<FormValues>();

  return (
    <Controller
      name="tier"
      control={control}
      render={({ field, fieldState }) => (
        <BpDropdown
          label="Service tier"
          options={options}
          value={field.value}
          onValueChange={(next) => field.onChange(next)}
          hasError={fieldState.invalid}
          message={fieldState.error?.message}
          messageType={fieldState.error ? "error" : undefined}
        />
      )}
    />
  );
}
```

## TanStack Form example

```tsx
import { useForm } from "@tanstack/react-form";
import { BpTextArea } from "@netapp/blueprint";

type FormValues = { description: string };

export function DescriptionForm() {
  const form = useForm<FormValues>({
    defaultValues: { description: "" },
  });

  return (
    <form.Field name="description">
      {(field) => (
        <BpTextArea
          label="Description"
          value={field.state.value}
          onChange={(event) => field.handleChange(event.currentTarget.value)}
          hasError={field.state.meta.errors.length > 0}
          message={field.state.meta.errors[0]}
          messageType={field.state.meta.errors.length > 0 ? "error" : undefined}
        />
      )}
    </form.Field>
  );
}
```

## Callback guidance

- For native text inputs, prefer `onChange` when you need the DOM event, or `onValueChange` when you only need the next string.
- Do not pass both unless you intentionally need both notifications.
- Keep one source of truth for value (`value` for controlled, `defaultValue` for uncontrolled).
